<h2>Why “website cost” searches feel stressful in Michigan</h2>
<p>If you are a small business owner in Michigan, you have probably seen quotes that range from a friend’s nephew at $200 to an agency retainer that never ends. That spread is real, and it is not because everyone is trying to scam you. It is because “a website” can mean a three-page brochure, a ten-page site with booking, or a custom build with integrations. In 2026, customers still judge you on mobile speed, clear services, and whether your phone number is one tap away. This guide uses the same packaged pricing I publish on Webstar — no hidden discovery fee before you see numbers.</p>
<p>Metro Detroit owners often ask me after a slow season or right before a busy one: “What should I budget?” I answer with tiers because scope drives cost more than zip code. A plumber covering Warren and Sterling Heights needs different pages than a Royal Oak salon with a brand kit. Google Business Profile, Analytics, and Search Console setup is included on every Webstar package because a site without measurement and maps alignment is half a project.</p>

<h2>Webstar Simple Website: $350–$500 one-time</h2>
<p>Simple Website is the entry point I recommend for most solo operators and small teams launching for the first time. You get three responsive pages, a contact form, click-to-call and book-now buttons, custom design, and full Google setup with you as owner of every account. Turnaround target is about 24 hours after complete intake — logo, photos, and a short list of services go a long way toward hitting that timeline.</p>
<p>The price band ($350–$500) reflects copy complexity and how polished the photo set is, not arbitrary upselling. If you already have sharp iPhone photos of your shop on Woodward or your crew on a Macomb job site, we move faster. If you need help structuring services from scratch, we still stay inside the band unless you add pages beyond the three-page scope.</p>
<ul>
<li>Best for: guides, trades, cleaners, and local shops that need credibility fast</li>
<li>Includes: Google Business Profile create or claim, GA4, Search Console, 30-day post-launch fix window</li>
<li>You own: domain (your registrar), hosting choice, and all site files after handoff</li>
</ul>

<h2>Business Website: $900–$1,800 one-time</h2>
<p>When you outgrow three pages, Business Website is the next step. Expect roughly five to ten pages, booking or calendar integration, and a print and brand kit — two flyers, business card, letterhead, and email signature — so your truck wrap, front desk, and homepage match. Turnaround is typically 2–4 days depending on booking tool credentials and how many service areas you cover across Oakland and Wayne County.</p>
<p>Most Michigan service businesses land here when they need separate pages for commercial vs residential work, or when they want staff bios without cluttering the homepage. The $900–$1,800 range maps to integration count and content volume. We confirm in writing at intake so you are not guessing mid-build.</p>

<h2>Business Growth Website: $2,500+ one-time</h2>
<p>Custom growth builds start at $2,500 because they include deeper functionality: more pages, third-party integrations, custom features, and longer discovery. Think multi-location service rules, lead routing, or industry-specific calculators you approve — not gimmicks. These projects still include the same Google ownership model; they just take longer to spec and test.</p>
<p>If you are comparing bids above $2,500, ask each vendor what happens after launch: hosting, edits, and who holds the Analytics account. Webstar hands you keys on every tier. Growth builds often pair with Care Plans if you want monthly edits without hiring a full-time marketer.</p>

<h2>Website as a Service: $150 per month</h2>
<p>Not everyone wants a four-figure invoice before revenue catches up. Website as a Service spreads the build and ongoing care into $150 per month with a six-month minimum. Hosting, backups, monitoring, sixty minutes of edits, and a plain-English monthly report are included. After month six you own the files outright and can stay on the plan, switch to the $50 Care Plan, or export to your own host.</p>
<p>Total math matters: six months at $150 is $900 — comparable to the lower end of Business Website — but you also get hosting and maintenance during those months. For a new LLC in Livonia or a side business testing demand, that predictability beats putting $1,800 on a card before your first paying customer.</p>

<h2>What is not included (so you are not surprised)</h2>
<p>Package prices cover the site and Google setup listed on each tier. They do not include unlimited copywriting from scratch, professional photography shoots, or paid ad management. If you need a photographer, I am happy to recommend Michigan creatives, but their invoices are separate. Domain registration is yours — typically $15–$20 per year through Namecheap, Google Domains successors, or Cloudflare — and I register it in your name when we launch.</p>
<p>Email mailboxes at your domain (hello@yourbusiness.com) may use Google Workspace or Microsoft 365; those are third-party subscriptions. I can point DNS correctly during launch week, but the mailbox bill goes to you, which is how it should be for ownership.</p>

<h2>Hidden costs on DIY builders and cheap templates</h2>
<p>Wix and Squarespace promos hide renewal jumps and plugin fees. A “free” site that cannot use your domain without paying extra is a marketing funnel, not an asset. Template clutter also slows mobile load on I-75 commuters checking you at a red light. When owners add up three years of builder subscriptions, they often exceed a one-time $350–$500 Simple Website plus inexpensive hosting.</p>
<p>Time is the other hidden cost. Every hour you drag boxes in a builder is an hour not fishing, quoting jobs, or serving clients. Fixed packages trade your time for a defined deliverable list.</p>

<h2>How Michigan taxes and contracts usually work</h2>
<p>Webstar Business Services LLC is a Michigan company. Project fees are quoted in writing; Stripe handles invoicing. You receive files and account access documented at handoff. If you need a W-9 for your bookkeeper, ask during intake. I am not an accountant — talk to yours about expensing a website as a capital improvement vs marketing — but keep your agreement and paid invoice for their records.</p>

<h2>Choosing a tier without overbuying</h2>
<p>Start with outcomes: Do you need booking this month, or only phone calls? Do you serve one city or all of Macomb County? If calls are enough, Simple Website plus a tight Google profile beats a ten-page site you will not maintain. If you run promos every season and need print matched to web, Business Website saves you from hiring a separate designer for flyers.</p>
<p>You can upgrade later without throwing away work — we extend the same design system rather than rebooting on a new subdomain. That path is common for contractors who begin with three pages and add a commercial services page after their first big referral partner.</p>

